      ⍝ How much room a workspace has, and what happens when it runs out.
      ⍝ I-beam 22 reports the bytes still free. A clear workspace has the
      ⍝ whole of its size free; the default is 1048576 bytes, and --ws-size
      ⍝ sets a different one.
      ⌶22
1048576
      ⍝ A name costs its entry and its characters; a value costs a
      ⍝ descriptor, an entry per axis, and its elements -- eight bytes a
      ⍝ number, one a character. So a hundred numbers under a one-letter
      ⍝ name come to 829 bytes.
      A←⍳100
      ⌶22
1047747
      ⍝ Assigning over a name gives back what it held first, so replacing
      ⍝ the hundred numbers with one leaves only the small value behind.
      A←1
      ⌶22
1048543
      ⍝ Characters are a byte each, whatever they take in UTF-8: the
      ⍝ accounting is of characters, not of their encoding.
      T←'ABCDE'
      ⌶22
1048509
      ⍝ A defined function costs a descriptor, the names its header makes
      ⍝ local, and each body line as it was typed -- the body is held as
      ⍝ text and parsed when it runs, so text is what it costs.
      ∇R←A HYP B
[1]   R←((A*2)+B*2)*0.5
[2]   ∇
      ⌶22
1048434
      3 HYP 4
5
      ⍝ )CLEAR gives everything back.
      )CLEAR
CLEAR WS
      ⌶22
1048576
      ⍝ A value that will not fit in what is left is WS FULL, and nothing
      ⍝ is stored: the workspace is left exactly as it was. Two hundred
      ⍝ thousand numbers want 1600020 bytes, which the default size has
      ⍝ no room for.
      BIG←⍳200000
WS FULL
      BIG←⍳200000
      ^
      ⌶22
1048576
      ⍝ The name still holds nothing.
      BIG
VALUE ERROR
      BIG
      ^
      ⍝ What a workspace holds is bounded; what an expression builds on
      ⍝ the way to a result is not. This is the size of the workspace, not
      ⍝ of the machine.
      ⍴⍳200000
200000
